@page "/get-started"

<PageContainer Title="Get Started" Path="/get-started" Category="Introduction" Description="Create a twblazor Blazor Web App with the project template, or add twblazor to an existing Server or WebAssembly app." SeoTitle="Get Started | twblazor - Install Tailwind CSS Components for Blazor">
    <TwAlert Color="TwBlazor.Enums.Color.Primary" StartIcon="TwBlazor.Enums.Icon.Brush" Class="mb-6 ps-3">
        <p class="font-semibold">Start from the project template</p>
        <p>
            The <strong>Project template</strong> tab creates a complete app in one command: solution, pages, theme, Tailwind CSS and a dashboard, sidebar, navbar or blank layout. Use the other tabs to create the project yourself or add twblazor to an existing app, and the <a href="/theme" class="font-semibold underline">Theme builder</a> to change the default theme.
        </p>
    </TwAlert>

    <TwTabContainer AriaLabel="Setup method">
        <TwTab Label="Project template">
            <PageCard Title="Choose a starting point" NoMarginTop>
                <p class="mb-4 max-w-2xl text-sm leading-6 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                    Pick the render mode and starting layout. The commands below update to match.
                </p>

                <h3 class="mb-2 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Render mode</h3>
                <TwGrid Cols="1" ColsSm="3" Gap="2">
                    <button type="button"
                            aria-pressed="@(selectedInteractivity == "server" ? "true" : "false")"
                            class="@(InteractivityCardClass("server"))"
                            @onclick="SelectServer">
                        <span class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
                            <span class="text-sm font-semibold">Server</span>
                            @if (selectedInteractivity == "server")
                            {
                                <TwIcon Icon="TwBlazor.Enums.Icon.Check_Circle_Fill" Class="text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-300" />
                            }
                        </span>
                        <span class="mt-1 block text-xs leading-5 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Runs interactivity on the server over a SignalR connection.</span>
                    </button>

                    <button type="button"
                            aria-pressed="@(selectedInteractivity == "wasm" ? "true" : "false")"
                            class="@(InteractivityCardClass("wasm"))"
                            @onclick="SelectWebAssembly">
                        <span class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
                            <span class="text-sm font-semibold">WebAssembly</span>
                            @if (selectedInteractivity == "wasm")
                            {
                                <TwIcon Icon="TwBlazor.Enums.Icon.Check_Circle_Fill" Class="text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-300" />
                            }
                        </span>
                        <span class="mt-1 block text-xs leading-5 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Runs interactivity in the browser with WebAssembly.</span>
                    </button>

                    <button type="button"
                            aria-pressed="@(selectedInteractivity == "auto" ? "true" : "false")"
                            class="@(InteractivityCardClass("auto"))"
                            @onclick="SelectAuto">
                        <span class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
                            <span class="text-sm font-semibold">Auto</span>
                            @if (selectedInteractivity == "auto")
                            {
                                <TwIcon Icon="TwBlazor.Enums.Icon.Check_Circle_Fill" Class="text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-300" />
                            }
                        </span>
                        <span class="mt-1 block text-xs leading-5 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Starts on the server, then switches to WebAssembly once it has loaded.</span>
                    </button>
                </TwGrid>

                <h3 class="mt-5 mb-2 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Layout</h3>
                <TwGrid Cols="1" ColsSm="2" ColsXl="4" Gap="3">
                    <button type="button"
                            aria-pressed="@(selectedLayout == "dashboard" ? "true" : "false")"
                            class="@(LayoutCardClass("dashboard"))"
                            @onclick="SelectDashboard">
                        <span class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
                            <span class="text-sm font-semibold">Dashboard</span>
                            @if (selectedLayout == "dashboard")
                            {
                                <TwIcon Icon="TwBlazor.Enums.Icon.Check_Circle_Fill" Class="text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-300" />
                            }
                        </span>
                        <span class="@(LayoutPreviewContainerClass("dashboard"))" aria-hidden="true">
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 72" class="h-full w-full" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" focusable="false">
                                <rect x="2" y="2" width="116" height="68" rx="5" />
                                <path d="M2 15h116M30 15v55" />
                                <path d="M4 17h24v51H4z" fill="currentColor" stroke="none" opacity=".12" />
                                <path d="M8 25h16M8 31h16M8 37h12M8 43h14" stroke-linecap="round" />
                                <rect x="35" y="21" width="23" height="12" rx="2" />
                                <rect x="62" y="21" width="23" height="12" rx="2" />
                                <rect x="89" y="21" width="25" height="12" rx="2" />
                                <rect x="35" y="39" width="51" height="26" rx="2" />
                                <rect x="89" y="39" width="25" height="26" rx="2" />
                                <path d="m39 58 10-8 8 5 10-10 7 4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                            </svg>
                        </span>
                        <span class="mt-3 block text-xs leading-5 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Sidebar, top bar, KPI cards and charts.</span>
                    </button>

                    <button type="button"
                            aria-pressed="@(selectedLayout == "sidebar" ? "true" : "false")"
                            class="@(LayoutCardClass("sidebar"))"
                            @onclick="SelectSidebar">
                        <span class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
                            <span class="text-sm font-semibold">Sidebar</span>
                            @if (selectedLayout == "sidebar")
                            {
                                <TwIcon Icon="TwBlazor.Enums.Icon.Check_Circle_Fill" Class="text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-300" />
                            }
                        </span>
                        <span class="@(LayoutPreviewContainerClass("sidebar"))" aria-hidden="true">
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 72" class="h-full w-full" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" focusable="false">
                                <rect x="2" y="2" width="116" height="68" rx="5" />
                                <path d="M2 2h26v68H2z" fill="currentColor" stroke="none" opacity=".12" />
                                <path d="M28 2v68" />
                                <path d="M7 12h16M7 19h16M7 26h12M7 33h14" stroke-linecap="round" />
                                <rect x="35" y="9" width="78" height="11" rx="2" />
                                <rect x="35" y="26" width="35" height="36" rx="2" />
                                <rect x="76" y="26" width="37" height="18" rx="2" />
                                <rect x="76" y="49" width="37" height="13" rx="2" />
                            </svg>
                        </span>
                        <span class="mt-3 block text-xs leading-5 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Grouped sidebar navigation with a focused content area.</span>
                    </button>

                    <button type="button"
                            aria-pressed="@(selectedLayout == "navbar" ? "true" : "false")"
                            class="@(LayoutCardClass("navbar"))"
                            @onclick="SelectNavbar">
                        <span class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
                            <span class="text-sm font-semibold">Navbar</span>
                            @if (selectedLayout == "navbar")
                            {
                                <TwIcon Icon="TwBlazor.Enums.Icon.Check_Circle_Fill" Class="text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-300" />
                            }
                        </span>
                        <span class="@(LayoutPreviewContainerClass("navbar"))" aria-hidden="true">
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 72" class="h-full w-full" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" focusable="false">
                                <rect x="2" y="2" width="116" height="68" rx="5" />
                                <path d="M2 16h116" />
                                <path d="M2 2h116v14H2z" fill="currentColor" stroke="none" opacity=".12" />
                                <path d="M8 9h15M29 9h10M44 9h10" stroke-linecap="round" />
                                <rect x="35" y="25" width="51" height="18" rx="2" />
                                <rect x="90" y="25" width="22" height="18" rx="2" />
                                <rect x="35" y="49" width="23" height="13" rx="2" />
                                <rect x="62" y="49" width="23" height="13" rx="2" />
                                <rect x="90" y="49" width="22" height="13" rx="2" />
                            </svg>
                        </span>
                        <span class="mt-3 block text-xs leading-5 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Top navigation bar with a wide content area.</span>
                    </button>

                    <button type="button"
                            aria-pressed="@(selectedLayout == "blank" ? "true" : "false")"
                            class="@(LayoutCardClass("blank"))"
                            @onclick="SelectBlank">
                        <span class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
                            <span class="text-sm font-semibold">Blank</span>
                            @if (selectedLayout == "blank")
                            {
                                <TwIcon Icon="TwBlazor.Enums.Icon.Check_Circle_Fill" Class="text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-300" />
                            }
                        </span>
                        <span class="@(LayoutPreviewContainerClass("blank"))" aria-hidden="true">
                            <svg viewBox="0 0 120 72" class="h-full w-full" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" focusable="false">
                                <rect x="2" y="2" width="116" height="68" rx="5" />
                                <rect x="37" y="25" width="46" height="8" rx="2" fill="currentColor" stroke="none" opacity=".12" />
                                <path d="M43 40h34M48 48h24" stroke-linecap="round" />
                            </svg>
                        </span>
                        <span class="mt-3 block text-xs leading-5 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">Centered Hello world page with a link to the docs.</span>
                    </button>
                </TwGrid>

                <span class="sr-only" role="status" aria-live="polite">Selected render mode: @SelectedInteractivityLabel; selected layout: @SelectedLayoutLabel</span>

                <h3 class="mt-5 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Install and create in one line</h3>
                <p class="mb-3 mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                    Install the templates tool and create the app in one PowerShell line. This works even if you have not installed <code>twblazor.templates</code> yet:
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@TemplateOneLineCommand" Language="powershell" />
                <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                    Use <code>;</code> rather than <code>&amp;&amp;</code> on Windows PowerShell 5.1. PowerShell 7 accepts either.
                </p>
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="1. Install the templates tool">
                <p class="mb-3">
                    The <code>twblazor.templates</code> .NET tool installs the project templates and creates a complete app: the solution, pages, theme, Tailwind build and a dashboard, sidebar, navbar or blank layout. Install it once:
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@templateInstallTool" Language="powershell" />
                <p class="my-3">
                    The command it puts on your PATH is <code>twblazor</code>; the package is <code>twblazor.templates</code>. This is a .NET tool, so install it with <code>dotnet tool install</code>, not <code>dotnet new install</code>.
                </p>
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="2. Create the app">
                <p class="mb-3">
                    This creates a <strong class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">@SelectedInteractivityLabel</strong> app with the
                    <strong class="text-gray-900 dark:text-white">@SelectedLayoutLabel</strong> layout. It installs the templates, creates the solution, restores packages and builds the Tailwind stylesheet. Replace <code>MyApp</code> with your own project's name:
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@TemplateCreateCommand" Language="powershell" />
                <p class="my-3">
                    Change the render mode or layout in the first card and this command updates automatically. <code>twblazor new</code> is the only command you need after the tool is installed. <code>twblazor install</code> only registers the templates with <code>dotnet new</code>; it does not create an app.
                </p>
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="3. Choose the render mode and layout">
                <p class="mb-3">
                    The first card sets the render mode and layout used by the commands above. Every option can also be passed in any order:
                </p>
                <TwTable Id="template-options" Hoverable FixedLayout>
                    <Caption>Options accepted by the twblazor new command</Caption>
                    <ColumnGroup>
                        <TwTableColumn Width="11rem" />
                        <TwTableColumn Width="22rem" />
                        <TwTableColumn Width="8rem" />
                    </ColumnGroup>
                    <TableHeader>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 whitespace-nowrap">Option</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3">Values</th>
                            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3">Default</th>
                        </tr>
                    </TableHeader>
                    <TableBody>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3 text-start font-medium whitespace-nowrap"><code>-i</code>, <code>--interactivity</code></th>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3 whitespace-nowrap"><code>server</code>, <code>wasm</code> (or <code>webassembly</code>), <code>auto</code></td>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3 whitespace-nowrap"><code>server</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3 text-start font-medium whitespace-nowrap"><code>-t</code>, <code>--template</code></th>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3 whitespace-nowrap"><code>dashboard</code>, <code>sidebar</code>, <code>navbar</code>, <code>blank</code></td>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3 whitespace-nowrap"><code>dashboard</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3 text-start font-medium whitespace-nowrap"><code>-o</code>, <code>--output</code></th>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3">Folder to create the app in</td>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3"><code>./MyApp</code></td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3 text-start font-medium whitespace-nowrap"><code>--no-restore</code></th>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3">Skip <code>dotnet restore</code></td>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3">Restore runs</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3 text-start font-medium whitespace-nowrap"><code>--no-npm</code></th>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3">Skip <code>npm install</code> and the Tailwind build</td>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3">Both run</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3 text-start font-medium whitespace-nowrap"><code>--force</code></th>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3">Write into a folder that is not empty</td>
                            <td class="px-4 py-3">Refuses</td>
                        </tr>
                    </TableBody>
                </TwTable>
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="4. Run it">
                <p class="mb-3">
                    The command prints the same instructions when it finishes. Build the HTTP launch profile the first time so you do not need a development certificate just to preview:
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@templateRun" Language="powershell" />
                <p class="my-3">
                    Open <code>MyApp.slnx</code> in Visual Studio to work on it there. The generated <code>.vsconfig</code> asks for the ASP.NET and web development workload, which is all the app needs.
                </p>
                <p>
                    For WebAssembly and Auto, run the host project (<code>MyApp</code>), not <code>MyApp.Client</code>.
                </p>
            </PageCard>

        </TwTab>

        <TwTab Label="Blazor Server">
            <PageCard Title="1. Create the project" NoMarginTop>
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    Scaffold a new Blazor Web App using the default Interactive Server render mode. This creates a single project. Replace <code>{YourProjectName}</code> with your own project's name.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@serverCreateProject" Language="bash" />
                <p Class="my-3">
                    This only scaffolds the project itself, so you won't see a <code>.sln</code> file yet - that's expected. If you want one, e.g. to open the project in Visual Studio or Rider, create it with:
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@serverCreateSolution" Language="bash" />
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="2. Install the twblazor NuGet package">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    Install the <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/twblazor" target="_blank">twblazor NuGet package</a> in the <code>{YourProjectName}</code> project.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@serverInstallPackage" Language="bash" />
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="3. Add to App.razor">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    In <code>{YourProjectName}/Components/App.razor</code>, include two stylesheet links along with the default content. The first is for TwIcon, which uses Bootstrap Icons - while you can link to the Bootstrap Icons CDN directly, we include the icons in our package for convenience. The second is for <strong>your own</strong> Tailwind build, which will contain both TwBlazor's classes and the theme classes you configure in step 6. It doesn't exist yet, so it'll 404 until you build it in step 8; that's expected.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@appHeadExample" />
                <p Class="my-3">
                    At the bottom of the body you'll also need to include the links to twblazor.js.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@serverAppScriptsExample" />
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="4. Add to _Imports.razor">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    In <code>{YourProjectName}/Components/_Imports.razor</code> you'll need to add references to the following:
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@importsExample" />
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="5. Add the TwBlazor providers">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    TwBlazor toasts and dialogs require you to add the following to the top of <code>{YourProjectName}/Components/Layout/MainLayout.razor</code>.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@CodeExamples.Get("GetStartedProviders")" />
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="6. Add a TwTheme">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    TwBlazor requires you to set up a few simple configuration classes for theming. Since Tailwind CSS cannot compile classes from .DLLs, you configure the classes in your own project, where your own Tailwind build can see them. Create a <code>Theme.cs</code> file at the root of the <code>{YourProjectName}</code> project.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@CodeExamples.Get("GetStartedTheme")" Language="csharp" Class="max-h-100 overflow-y-scroll" />
                <TwAlert Color="TwBlazor.Enums.Color.Primary" StartIcon="TwBlazor.Enums.Icon.Brush" Class="mt-3 ps-3">
                    This is the default theme. To use your own brand colors or only the components you use, <a href="/theme" class="font-semibold underline">build your own Theme.cs</a> instead, and register it with <code>Theme.CreateTheme</code> in step 7.
                </TwAlert>
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="7. Register TwBlazor services">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    In <code>{YourProjectName}/Program.cs</code>, register TwBlazor's services using the theme you just configured.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@CodeExamples.Get("GetStartedDependencyInjection")" Language="csharp" />
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="8. Build your Tailwind CSS">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    TwBlazor doesn't ship a pre-built stylesheet - since Tailwind can't compile classes from a .DLL, every class TwBlazor's components use is also expressed as a literal in the <code>Theme.cs</code> you just wrote, so your own Tailwind build produces the whole thing in one pass. Install the <a href="https://tailwindcss.com/docs/installation/tailwind-cli" target="_blank">Tailwind CLI</a> from the solution root (the folder containing <code>{YourProjectName}.csproj</code>):
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@serverTailwindInstall" Language="bash" />
                <p Class="my-3">
                    Create an input stylesheet at <code>{YourProjectName}/wwwroot/css/input.css</code>. The <code>@@source</code> directives tell Tailwind to scan your razor components and <code>Theme.cs</code> for classes to generate. The keyframes at the bottom power <code>TwSkeleton</code>'s <code>Wave</code> variant - a shimmer effect Tailwind has no utility for, so it has to be real CSS rather than a class Tailwind can generate. Skip it if you don't use that variant:
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@serverTailwindInput" Language="css" />
                <p Class="my-3">
                    Then build it to <code>{YourProjectName}/wwwroot/css/output.css</code>, the file already referenced by the <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> tag you added to App.razor in step 3:
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@serverTailwindBuild" Language="bash" />
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="9. Try it out">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    Add a test page to confirm everything is wired up. Create <code>{YourProjectName}/Components/Pages/ComponentTest.razor</code>:
                </p>
                <ExampleCodeBlock Razor="@serverComponentTestPageRazor" CSharp="@serverComponentTestPageCode" />
                <p Class="my-3">
                    Run the app and go to <code>/component-test</code>. You should see a green success alert, a button whose label counts up each click, and three interactive switches.
                </p>
            </PageCard>
        </TwTab>

        <TwTab Label="Blazor WebAssembly">
            <PageCard Title="1. Create the project" NoMarginTop>
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    Scaffold a new Blazor Web App with WebAssembly interactivity. Unlike the Server model, this creates <strong>two</strong> projects side by side. Replace <code>{YourProjectName}</code> with your own project's name.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@wasmCreateProject" Language="bash" />
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@wasmProjectStructure" Language="text" Class="my-3" />
                <p Class="my-3">
                    Every step below tells you which of the two projects a file belongs in.
                </p>
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="2. Install the twblazor NuGet package">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    Install the <a href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/twblazor" target="_blank">twblazor NuGet package</a> in <strong>both</strong> projects. <code>{YourProjectName}.Client</code> needs it because the interactive layout and providers are compiled into it; <code>{YourProjectName}</code> needs it too because it references <code>{YourProjectName}.Client</code>'s <code>Theme</code> class and still renders those components on the server before the WebAssembly bundle takes over.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@wasmInstallPackage" Language="bash" />
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="3. Add to App.razor">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    App.razor always lives in the host project, even in the WebAssembly model. In <code>{YourProjectName}/Components/App.razor</code>, add the same two stylesheet links as before - the Bootstrap Icons stylesheet, and a link to your own project's Tailwind build, which won't exist until step 8.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@appHeadExample" />
                <p Class="my-3">
                    At the bottom of the body, include the twblazor.js script. Note the render mode is <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code> instead of <code>InteractiveServer</code>.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@wasmAppScriptsExample" />
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="4. Add to _Imports.razor">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    Add the following to <code>{YourProjectName}.Client/_Imports.razor</code>, since that's where your interactive components (like MainLayout.razor) live:
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@importsExample" />
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="5. Add the TwBlazor providers to MainLayout.razor">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    This is the one step that differs structurally from the Server model. TwToastProvider and TwDialogProvider rely on JS interop, so they must run on a component that supports interactive rendering. App.razor is always statically rendered, so the providers can't go there. Instead, add them to the top of <code>{YourProjectName}.Client/Layout/MainLayout.razor</code> - only code compiled into <code>{YourProjectName}.Client</code> can run under WebAssembly.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@wasmMainLayoutExample" />
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="6. Add a TwTheme">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    Since Tailwind CSS cannot compile classes from .DLLs, you configure the theme's classes in your own project. Create a <code>Theme.cs</code> file at the root of the <code>{YourProjectName}.Client</code> project - not <code>{YourProjectName}</code> - since that's where the components using this theme are compiled.
                </p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@CodeExamples.Get("GetStartedTheme")" Language="csharp" Class="max-h-100 overflow-y-scroll" />
                <TwAlert Color="TwBlazor.Enums.Color.Primary" StartIcon="TwBlazor.Enums.Icon.Brush" Class="mt-3 ps-3">
                    This is the default theme. To use your own brand colors or only the components you use, <a href="/theme" class="font-semibold underline">build your own Theme.cs</a> instead, and register it with <code>Theme.CreateTheme</code> in step 7.
                </TwAlert>
            </PageCard>

            <PageCard Title="7. Register TwBlazor services">
                <p Class="mb-3">
                    Register TwBlazor's services in <strong>both</strong> Program.cs files, pointing at the <code>Theme</code> class you just created in <code>{YourProjectName}.Client</code>.
                </p>
                <p Class="mb-3"><code>{YourProjectName}.Client/Program.cs</code>:</p>
                <TwCodeBlock Content="@wasmClientProgramExample" Language="csharp" />
                <p Class="my-3"><code>{YourProjectName}/Program.cs</code> - since <code>Theme</code> lives in the other project, add a <code>using</code> for it:</p>
